1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,436
It's Friday afternoon in nineteen sixty-two.

2
00:00:03,616 --> 00:00:06,479
Your husband comes through the front door,

3
00:00:06,479 --> 00:00:08,252
hangs his hat on the hook,

4
00:00:08,252 --> 00:00:10,637
and puts an envelope on the kitchen

5
00:00:10,637 --> 00:00:11,046
table.

6
00:00:11,226 --> 00:00:13,130
Or maybe it's your own envelope,

7
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:15,033
from the bank or the store where

8
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:15,569
you work.

9
00:00:15,749 --> 00:00:18,647
Either way, everybody in the house knows

10
00:00:18,647 --> 00:00:19,951
what Friday means.

11
00:00:20,131 --> 00:00:20,782
Payday.

12
00:00:21,132 --> 00:00:23,084
Tonight we're going to follow that one

13
00:00:23,084 --> 00:00:23,546
paycheck.

14
00:00:23,726 --> 00:00:25,063
Where it came from,

15
00:00:25,063 --> 00:00:26,117
how big it was,

16
00:00:26,117 --> 00:00:28,649
and exactly where every dollar went.

17
00:00:28,829 --> 00:00:30,710
The rent or the mortgage.

18
00:00:30,890 --> 00:00:31,946
The groceries.

19
00:00:32,126 --> 00:00:32,765
The car.

20
00:00:32,945 --> 00:00:33,700
The doctor.

21
00:00:33,880 --> 00:00:34,808
The church plate.

22
00:00:34,988 --> 00:00:36,819
And the little bit left over at

23
00:00:36,819 --> 00:00:38,355
the end, if there was any.

24
00:00:38,706 --> 00:00:41,729
Because the numbers from nineteen sixty-two tell

25
00:00:41,729 --> 00:00:43,744
a story that might surprise you.

26
00:00:43,924 --> 00:00:46,420
Some things cost almost nothing.

27
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:48,690
Some things cost a great deal more

28
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:49,735
than you'd think.

29
00:00:49,915 --> 00:00:51,048
And one paycheck,

30
00:00:51,048 --> 00:00:53,113
for a lot of American families,

31
00:00:53,113 --> 00:00:55,778
was enough to carry the whole household.

32
00:00:55,958 --> 00:00:57,456
Let's open the envelope.

33
00:00:57,806 --> 00:00:59,492
In nineteen sixty-two,

34
00:00:59,492 --> 00:01:02,481
the median family income in America was

35
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:05,317
a little under six thousand dollars a

36
00:01:05,317 --> 00:01:05,700
year.

37
00:01:05,880 --> 00:01:08,553
That works out to roughly one hundred

38
00:01:08,553 --> 00:01:10,503
and fifteen dollars a week,

39
00:01:10,503 --> 00:01:11,441
before taxes.

40
00:01:11,621 --> 00:01:13,441
In most of those families,

41
00:01:13,441 --> 00:01:15,331
it was mostly one paycheck,

42
00:01:15,331 --> 00:01:16,801
earned by the father,

43
00:01:16,801 --> 00:01:18,971
while the mother ran the house.

44
00:01:19,321 --> 00:01:21,633
A factory worker with a union job

45
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:23,875
might take home around two and a

46
00:01:23,875 --> 00:01:25,346
half dollars an hour.

47
00:01:25,526 --> 00:01:28,987
A schoolteacher earned around five thousand dollars

48
00:01:28,987 --> 00:01:29,462
a year.

49
00:01:29,642 --> 00:01:31,946
A young woman in an office might

50
00:01:31,946 --> 00:01:34,611
earn sixty or seventy dollars a week.

51
00:01:34,791 --> 00:01:36,723
And the federal minimum wage,

52
00:01:36,723 --> 00:01:38,523
for the jobs at the bottom,

53
00:01:38,523 --> 00:01:40,855
was one dollar and fifteen cents an

54
00:01:40,855 --> 00:01:41,188
hour.

55
00:01:41,538 --> 00:01:43,476
Taxes came out first.

56
00:01:43,656 --> 00:01:45,164
Federal income tax,

57
00:01:45,164 --> 00:01:46,750
and Social Security,

58
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:50,083
which in nineteen sixty-two took three and

59
00:01:50,083 --> 00:01:52,542
one-eighth percent of your pay,

60
00:01:52,542 --> 00:01:53,653
up to a limit.

61
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:55,771
Many states took a bit more.

62
00:01:55,951 --> 00:01:58,019
What was left went home in the

63
00:01:58,019 --> 00:02:00,568
envelope, or more and more often now,

64
00:02:00,568 --> 00:02:02,498
as a paper check you took to

65
00:02:02,498 --> 00:02:04,496
the bank on Saturday morning.

66
00:02:04,847 --> 00:02:05,540
At the bank,

67
00:02:05,540 --> 00:02:07,679
you stood in line with your passbook.

68
00:02:07,859 --> 00:02:10,250
The teller wrote your deposit in by

69
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:11,411
hand, stamped it,

70
00:02:11,411 --> 00:02:13,733
and slid it back across the marble

71
00:02:13,733 --> 00:02:14,280
counter.

72
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:16,877
Some of the money went into checking,

73
00:02:16,877 --> 00:02:17,792
for the bills.

74
00:02:17,972 --> 00:02:19,368
And if you were careful,

75
00:02:19,368 --> 00:02:21,229
a few dollars went into savings,

76
00:02:21,229 --> 00:02:23,497
where it earned a little interest every

77
00:02:23,497 --> 00:02:23,962
quarter.

78
00:02:24,313 --> 00:02:26,029
For the man of the house,

79
00:02:26,029 --> 00:02:28,775
the paycheck said something about who he

80
00:02:28,775 --> 00:02:29,049
was.

81
00:02:29,229 --> 00:02:31,872
Bringing home enough to support a wife

82
00:02:31,872 --> 00:02:34,098
and children on one income was a

83
00:02:34,098 --> 00:02:36,671
point of pride in nineteen sixty-two.

84
00:02:36,851 --> 00:02:39,326
Men compared notes quietly at the barber

85
00:02:39,326 --> 00:02:40,996
shop and the bowling alley,

86
00:02:40,996 --> 00:02:42,790
and a raise of five dollars a

87
00:02:42,790 --> 00:02:44,894
week was news worth sharing at the

88
00:02:44,894 --> 00:02:45,698
dinner table.

89
00:02:46,049 --> 00:02:48,231
The biggest bill was the house.

90
00:02:48,411 --> 00:02:49,793
In the early sixties,

91
00:02:49,793 --> 00:02:52,227
the typical new house sold for around

92
00:02:52,227 --> 00:02:53,938
eighteen thousand dollars.

93
00:02:54,118 --> 00:02:55,969
With a thirty-year mortgage,

94
00:02:55,969 --> 00:02:58,879
often backed by the government for veterans,

95
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:01,458
the monthly payment might be around one

96
00:03:01,458 --> 00:03:04,567
hundred dollars, including taxes and insurance.

97
00:03:04,917 --> 00:03:06,641
That meant about a quarter of the

98
00:03:06,641 --> 00:03:08,051
paycheck went to the house.

99
00:03:08,231 --> 00:03:09,411
The rule of thumb,

100
00:03:09,411 --> 00:03:11,247
which every banker repeated,

101
00:03:11,247 --> 00:03:13,607
was that a family should never spend

102
00:03:13,607 --> 00:03:15,640
more than one week's pay out of

103
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,148
every month on housing.

104
00:03:17,328 --> 00:03:18,839
In nineteen sixty-two,

105
00:03:18,839 --> 00:03:21,519
most families could actually follow it.

106
00:03:21,869 --> 00:03:24,207
Renters in the city might pay seventy

107
00:03:24,207 --> 00:03:26,229
or eighty dollars a month for an

108
00:03:26,229 --> 00:03:26,861
apartment.

109
00:03:27,041 --> 00:03:29,223
The landlord often lived in the same

110
00:03:29,223 --> 00:03:31,709
building, collected the rent in person on

111
00:03:31,709 --> 00:03:33,103
the first of the month,

112
00:03:33,103 --> 00:03:34,800
and wrote you a receipt in a

113
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:35,528
little book.

114
00:03:35,708 --> 00:03:38,610
Heat and hot water were usually included.

115
00:03:38,961 --> 00:03:40,551
Then there were the utilities.

116
00:03:40,731 --> 00:03:42,104
The electric bill,

117
00:03:42,104 --> 00:03:43,095
the gas bill,

118
00:03:43,095 --> 00:03:45,611
the water bill and the telephone,

119
00:03:45,611 --> 00:03:48,966
which was rented from the telephone company,

120
00:03:48,966 --> 00:03:49,729
not owned.

121
00:03:49,909 --> 00:03:52,482
Together, they might run around twenty or

122
00:03:52,482 --> 00:03:54,239
twenty-five dollars a month.

123
00:03:54,419 --> 00:03:57,378
Long-distance calls were the dangerous part,

124
00:03:57,378 --> 00:03:59,598
and every family had a rule about

125
00:03:59,598 --> 00:03:59,934
them.

126
00:04:00,284 --> 00:04:02,745
Heating the house was its own expense.

127
00:04:02,925 --> 00:04:05,498
Many homes still burned fuel oil,

128
00:04:05,498 --> 00:04:08,149
delivered by a truck that filled a

129
00:04:08,149 --> 00:04:09,786
tank in the basement.

130
00:04:09,966 --> 00:04:13,473
Some older houses still had coal furnaces.

131
00:04:13,653 --> 00:04:14,622
And in winter,

132
00:04:14,622 --> 00:04:16,699
the rule in a great many homes

133
00:04:16,699 --> 00:04:17,461
was simple.

134
00:04:17,641 --> 00:04:18,748
If you're cold,

135
00:04:18,748 --> 00:04:20,003
put on a sweater,

136
00:04:20,003 --> 00:04:22,366
and don't touch that thermostat.

137
00:04:22,716 --> 00:04:24,271
Next came the groceries.

138
00:04:24,451 --> 00:04:26,786
A family of four might spend around

139
00:04:26,786 --> 00:04:29,387
twenty to twenty-five dollars a week at

140
00:04:29,387 --> 00:04:30,454
the supermarket.

141
00:04:30,634 --> 00:04:32,473
That was nearly a fifth of the

142
00:04:32,473 --> 00:04:35,111
paycheck, a much bigger share than families

143
00:04:35,111 --> 00:04:35,847
spend today.

144
00:04:36,027 --> 00:04:38,803
Food was the second biggest bill in

145
00:04:38,803 --> 00:04:40,311
almost every house.

146
00:04:40,661 --> 00:04:42,228
Here's what that money bought.

147
00:04:42,408 --> 00:04:44,670
A loaf of white bread for around

148
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:45,589
twenty cents.

149
00:04:45,769 --> 00:04:48,730
A dozen eggs for around thirty cents.

150
00:04:48,910 --> 00:04:51,566
A pound of ground beef for around

151
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:52,532
fifty cents.

152
00:04:52,712 --> 00:04:55,578
A half-gallon of milk delivered to your

153
00:04:55,578 --> 00:04:57,709
door, or bought at the store,

154
00:04:57,709 --> 00:04:59,399
for around fifty cents.

155
00:04:59,579 --> 00:05:00,964
Coffee came in a can,

156
00:05:00,964 --> 00:05:03,206
and nobody had heard of spending a

157
00:05:03,206 --> 00:05:04,722
dollar on a single cup.

158
00:05:05,073 --> 00:05:06,779
Mothers stretched every dollar.

159
00:05:06,959 --> 00:05:09,328
They clipped coupons from the newspaper.

160
00:05:09,508 --> 00:05:12,407
They saved trading stamps from the supermarket,

161
00:05:12,407 --> 00:05:14,258
licked them into little books,

162
00:05:14,258 --> 00:05:16,356
and traded the books for a toaster

163
00:05:16,356 --> 00:05:16,973
or a lamp.

164
00:05:17,153 --> 00:05:19,125
They bought a whole chicken and made

165
00:05:19,125 --> 00:05:20,276
it last three nights.

166
00:05:20,456 --> 00:05:22,302
Nothing was ever thrown away.

167
00:05:22,652 --> 00:05:24,725
A lot of families also grew part

168
00:05:24,725 --> 00:05:25,891
of their own food.

169
00:05:26,071 --> 00:05:28,542
A vegetable garden behind the house,

170
00:05:28,542 --> 00:05:31,014
tomatoes and beans and squash in the

171
00:05:31,014 --> 00:05:33,622
summer, and jars of canned peaches and

172
00:05:33,622 --> 00:05:36,368
pickles lined up on basement shelves for

173
00:05:36,368 --> 00:05:37,123
the winter.

174
00:05:37,303 --> 00:05:38,458
In small towns,

175
00:05:38,458 --> 00:05:41,537
some families still kept chickens in the

176
00:05:41,537 --> 00:05:42,923
backyard for eggs.

177
00:05:43,273 --> 00:05:46,147
The milkman still came to many neighborhoods

178
00:05:46,147 --> 00:05:47,845
two or three times a week,

179
00:05:47,845 --> 00:05:50,392
leaving glass bottles on the front step

180
00:05:50,392 --> 00:05:51,829
in a little metal box.

181
00:05:52,009 --> 00:05:53,682
The bread man came too,

182
00:05:53,682 --> 00:05:54,701
in some towns,

183
00:05:54,701 --> 00:05:56,810
and the egg man from the farm

184
00:05:56,810 --> 00:05:57,756
outside town.

185
00:05:57,936 --> 00:05:59,256
You paid them weekly,

186
00:05:59,256 --> 00:06:01,206
in cash, or they kept a running

187
00:06:01,206 --> 00:06:02,777
tab in a little notebook.

188
00:06:03,128 --> 00:06:05,438
A dinner out was a real treat.

189
00:06:05,618 --> 00:06:07,565
A family might go to a diner

190
00:06:07,565 --> 00:06:09,513
or a cafeteria once a month,

191
00:06:09,513 --> 00:06:12,225
where a hamburger cost around thirty or

192
00:06:12,225 --> 00:06:13,060
forty cents.

193
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:15,875
And the new fast-food stands along the

194
00:06:15,875 --> 00:06:18,163
highway, with their golden signs,

195
00:06:18,163 --> 00:06:20,590
sold a hamburger for fifteen cents,

196
00:06:20,590 --> 00:06:22,947
which seemed almost too good to be

197
00:06:22,947 --> 00:06:23,294
true.

198
00:06:23,645 --> 00:06:25,663
After the house and the food came

199
00:06:25,663 --> 00:06:26,152
the car.

200
00:06:26,332 --> 00:06:29,457
A brand-new Chevrolet or Ford cost somewhere

201
00:06:29,457 --> 00:06:32,370
around two thousand five hundred dollars.

202
00:06:32,550 --> 00:06:34,755
Most families bought it on time,

203
00:06:34,755 --> 00:06:35,581
with a loan,

204
00:06:35,581 --> 00:06:38,338
and paid around sixty or seventy dollars

205
00:06:38,338 --> 00:06:39,992
a month for three years.

206
00:06:40,172 --> 00:06:42,428
A lot of families bought a good

207
00:06:42,428 --> 00:06:43,666
used car instead.

208
00:06:44,016 --> 00:06:46,879
Gasoline cost around thirty cents a gallon,

209
00:06:46,879 --> 00:06:49,076
and the man at the station pumped

210
00:06:49,076 --> 00:06:49,809
it for you.

211
00:06:49,989 --> 00:06:52,396
A family car might get twelve or

212
00:06:52,396 --> 00:06:54,276
fifteen miles per gallon,

213
00:06:54,276 --> 00:06:56,758
and nobody worried much about it.

214
00:06:56,938 --> 00:06:59,568
A Sunday drive to see the grandparents

215
00:06:59,568 --> 00:07:01,437
might cost a dollar in gas,

216
00:07:01,437 --> 00:07:02,476
there and back.

217
00:07:02,826 --> 00:07:05,144
The car was expensive to keep up,

218
00:07:05,144 --> 00:07:05,635
though.

219
00:07:05,815 --> 00:07:07,940
Tires wore out quickly.

220
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:11,278
The oil was changed every thousand miles.

221
00:07:11,458 --> 00:07:12,939
And every few years,

222
00:07:12,939 --> 00:07:15,605
the family car needed a new muffler,

223
00:07:15,605 --> 00:07:16,641
a new battery,

224
00:07:16,641 --> 00:07:18,715
or a whole new transmission,

225
00:07:18,715 --> 00:07:21,381
which could eat a month's savings in

226
00:07:21,381 --> 00:07:22,418
one afternoon.

227
00:07:22,768 --> 00:07:25,714
Car insurance was cheaper than today but

228
00:07:25,714 --> 00:07:27,039
still a real bill,

229
00:07:27,039 --> 00:07:29,470
and many families paid it twice a

230
00:07:29,470 --> 00:07:29,838
year.

231
00:07:30,018 --> 00:07:32,584
Most people didn't carry much coverage.

232
00:07:32,764 --> 00:07:35,160
And plenty of fathers did their own

233
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:37,145
repairs on Saturday mornings,

234
00:07:37,145 --> 00:07:39,335
with the hood up in the driveway

235
00:07:39,335 --> 00:07:41,662
and a son handing him the wrenches

236
00:07:41,662 --> 00:07:42,621
one at a time.

237
00:07:42,971 --> 00:07:45,327
Then came all the smaller things.

238
00:07:45,507 --> 00:07:48,102
Clothing, much of it sewn at home

239
00:07:48,102 --> 00:07:51,090
or handed down from older brothers and

240
00:07:51,090 --> 00:07:51,719
sisters.

241
00:07:51,899 --> 00:07:53,333
A visit to the doctor,

242
00:07:53,333 --> 00:07:55,288
which might cost five dollars,

243
00:07:55,288 --> 00:07:57,309
paid in cash at the front desk.

244
00:07:57,489 --> 00:07:58,929
The family dentist.

245
00:07:59,109 --> 00:07:59,852
Haircuts.

246
00:08:00,032 --> 00:08:02,748
Shoes resoled at the cobbler instead of

247
00:08:02,748 --> 00:08:03,584
thrown away.

248
00:08:03,935 --> 00:08:07,076
A first-class stamp cost four cents,

249
00:08:07,076 --> 00:08:09,693
until it went up to five cents

250
00:08:09,693 --> 00:08:12,747
in January of nineteen sixty-three.

251
00:08:12,927 --> 00:08:14,877
The newspaper cost a dime.

252
00:08:15,057 --> 00:08:17,362
A movie ticket cost well under a

253
00:08:17,362 --> 00:08:17,867
dollar.

254
00:08:18,047 --> 00:08:20,113
And a family could spend a whole

255
00:08:20,113 --> 00:08:22,244
Saturday at the county fair for a

256
00:08:22,244 --> 00:08:24,827
few dollars, including the cotton candy.

257
00:08:25,177 --> 00:08:28,080
There was the collection plate on Sunday,

258
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:30,558
which many families took seriously,

259
00:08:30,558 --> 00:08:33,107
and often a weekly envelope with the

260
00:08:33,107 --> 00:08:34,523
family's name on it.

261
00:08:34,703 --> 00:08:36,975
There were the insurance payments,

262
00:08:36,975 --> 00:08:39,182
to a man who sometimes came right

263
00:08:39,182 --> 00:08:41,053
to the door to collect them.

264
00:08:41,233 --> 00:08:43,622
And there was the Christmas club at

265
00:08:43,622 --> 00:08:45,464
the bank, a dollar or two a

266
00:08:45,464 --> 00:08:47,852
week saved all year for presents in

267
00:08:47,852 --> 00:08:48,466
December.

268
00:08:48,817 --> 00:08:50,100
And there was credit,

269
00:08:50,100 --> 00:08:51,812
though not as much as today.

270
00:08:51,992 --> 00:08:54,882
Department stores gave charge accounts to good

271
00:08:54,882 --> 00:08:55,510
customers.

272
00:08:55,690 --> 00:08:58,009
A few people carried a new kind

273
00:08:58,009 --> 00:09:00,703
of card from a company called Diners

274
00:09:00,703 --> 00:09:01,077
Club.

275
00:09:01,257 --> 00:09:03,649
But most families still paid cash,

276
00:09:03,649 --> 00:09:06,322
and if they couldn't afford something,

277
00:09:06,322 --> 00:09:08,362
they waited until they could.

278
00:09:08,712 --> 00:09:11,134
Some families bought big things on the

279
00:09:11,134 --> 00:09:12,218
installment plan.

280
00:09:12,398 --> 00:09:13,830
A new refrigerator,

281
00:09:13,830 --> 00:09:15,186
a washing machine,

282
00:09:15,186 --> 00:09:17,975
or a console television might be paid

283
00:09:17,975 --> 00:09:20,085
off at a few dollars a month

284
00:09:20,085 --> 00:09:21,140
for two years.

285
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:23,290
A payment book came with it,

286
00:09:23,290 --> 00:09:25,260
a little booklet of coupons,

287
00:09:25,260 --> 00:09:27,441
one torn out and mailed in with

288
00:09:27,441 --> 00:09:29,833
a check every month until the last

289
00:09:29,833 --> 00:09:30,747
one was gone.

290
00:09:31,098 --> 00:09:32,735
And what about vacations?

291
00:09:32,915 --> 00:09:34,194
For many families,

292
00:09:34,194 --> 00:09:36,539
the summer vacation was a week at

293
00:09:36,539 --> 00:09:37,463
a lake cabin,

294
00:09:37,463 --> 00:09:39,168
a trip to see relatives,

295
00:09:39,168 --> 00:09:41,229
or a long drive to a national

296
00:09:41,229 --> 00:09:44,000
park, with the whole family packed into

297
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,279
the station wagon.

298
00:09:45,459 --> 00:09:48,590
Motels cost around eight or ten dollars

299
00:09:48,590 --> 00:09:49,232
a night.

300
00:09:49,412 --> 00:09:52,368
Mother packed sandwiches so nobody had to

301
00:09:52,368 --> 00:09:54,242
pay for lunch on the road.

302
00:09:54,593 --> 00:09:56,491
So how did families keep it all

303
00:09:56,491 --> 00:09:57,042
straight?

304
00:09:57,222 --> 00:09:58,452
In a lot of houses,

305
00:09:58,452 --> 00:10:00,717
the answer was the envelope system.

306
00:10:00,897 --> 00:10:03,319
On payday, the mother sat at the

307
00:10:03,319 --> 00:10:06,270
kitchen table and divided the cash into

308
00:10:06,270 --> 00:10:07,027
envelopes.

309
00:10:07,207 --> 00:10:08,623
One for the house.

310
00:10:08,803 --> 00:10:10,243
One for groceries.

311
00:10:10,423 --> 00:10:11,630
One for the car.

312
00:10:11,810 --> 00:10:12,948
One for church.

313
00:10:13,128 --> 00:10:14,359
One for the doctor.

314
00:10:14,539 --> 00:10:15,658
One for savings,

315
00:10:15,658 --> 00:10:17,546
if there was anything left.

316
00:10:17,896 --> 00:10:20,650
When the grocery envelope was empty,

317
00:10:20,650 --> 00:10:23,711
the grocery shopping was done until next

318
00:10:23,711 --> 00:10:24,246
Friday.

319
00:10:24,426 --> 00:10:26,108
That was the whole budget,

320
00:10:26,108 --> 00:10:27,984
right there in a shoebox or a

321
00:10:27,984 --> 00:10:28,954
kitchen drawer.

322
00:10:29,134 --> 00:10:30,504
No spreadsheets.

323
00:10:30,684 --> 00:10:31,578
No apps.

324
00:10:31,758 --> 00:10:32,829
Just a pencil,

325
00:10:32,829 --> 00:10:34,434
a stack of envelopes,

326
00:10:34,434 --> 00:10:37,034
and a woman who knew exactly where

327
00:10:37,034 --> 00:10:38,411
every dollar went.

328
00:10:38,761 --> 00:10:41,596
Many fathers handed the whole envelope to

329
00:10:41,596 --> 00:10:44,502
their wives and received a small allowance

330
00:10:44,502 --> 00:10:45,608
back, for lunch,

331
00:10:45,608 --> 00:10:46,922
gas and the barber.

332
00:10:47,102 --> 00:10:48,496
It sounds strange now.

333
00:10:48,676 --> 00:10:50,484
But in nineteen sixty-two,

334
00:10:50,484 --> 00:10:52,640
in a great many American homes,

335
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:55,492
the person who actually managed the money

336
00:10:55,492 --> 00:10:56,536
was the mother.

337
00:10:56,886 --> 00:10:58,650
Was it easier back then?

338
00:10:58,830 --> 00:11:00,171
In some ways,

339
00:11:00,171 --> 00:11:00,583
yes.

340
00:11:00,763 --> 00:11:03,266
One paycheck could buy a house,

341
00:11:03,266 --> 00:11:05,527
a car, and three meals a day

342
00:11:05,527 --> 00:11:07,869
for a family of four or five.

343
00:11:08,049 --> 00:11:10,153
Housing took about a quarter of the

344
00:11:10,153 --> 00:11:10,394
pay.

345
00:11:10,574 --> 00:11:12,647
A young couple with a high school

346
00:11:12,647 --> 00:11:15,034
education could often afford a home of

347
00:11:15,034 --> 00:11:16,541
their own before thirty.

348
00:11:16,892 --> 00:11:18,393
In other ways,

349
00:11:18,393 --> 00:11:18,714
no.

350
00:11:18,894 --> 00:11:20,810
Families had far less.

351
00:11:20,990 --> 00:11:21,965
One car.

352
00:11:22,145 --> 00:11:23,353
One television.

353
00:11:23,533 --> 00:11:26,098
One bathroom, for six people.

354
00:11:26,278 --> 00:11:28,949
Clothes were mended until they fell apart.

355
00:11:29,129 --> 00:11:31,915
A long-distance call was an event.

356
00:11:32,095 --> 00:11:33,252
And many Americans,

357
00:11:33,252 --> 00:11:35,078
especially in the rural South,

358
00:11:35,078 --> 00:11:37,756
in the cities and in minority neighborhoods,

359
00:11:37,756 --> 00:11:40,435
never got anywhere near that median paycheck

360
00:11:40,435 --> 00:11:40,861
at all.

361
00:11:41,211 --> 00:11:42,929
What's certain is this.

362
00:11:43,109 --> 00:11:45,215
People knew the value of a dollar,

363
00:11:45,215 --> 00:11:46,826
because they could see it,

364
00:11:46,826 --> 00:11:48,561
hold it, and count it out on

365
00:11:48,561 --> 00:11:50,481
the kitchen table every Friday.

366
00:11:50,661 --> 00:11:53,055
That habit stayed with that generation for

367
00:11:53,055 --> 00:11:54,423
the rest of their lives.

368
00:11:54,603 --> 00:11:57,779
If your parents or grandparents saved string

369
00:11:57,779 --> 00:11:59,006
and rubber bands,

370
00:11:59,006 --> 00:12:00,234
now you know why.

371
00:12:00,584 --> 00:12:02,627
So tell us in the comments.

372
00:12:02,807 --> 00:12:05,023
What did your father or mother earn

373
00:12:05,023 --> 00:12:05,973
in the sixties,

374
00:12:05,973 --> 00:12:08,380
and what did your family's house cost?

375
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:09,906
We read every one.

376
00:12:10,086 --> 00:12:11,712
And stay with us for more.

377
00:12:11,892 --> 00:12:14,176
Next time, we'll take you to the

378
00:12:14,176 --> 00:12:16,674
main streets of small-town America,

379
00:12:16,674 --> 00:12:17,674
in full color.

380
00:12:17,854 --> 00:12:19,593
Thank you for spending this time with

381
00:12:19,593 --> 00:12:19,734
me.
